{
  // Vaipakam agent Worker — proactive notifications + operator
  // services + public Frames + Telegram bot + diagnostics record
  // + frontend-facing settings.
  // (#1651: "cross-chain monitoring" was listed here for the
  // buy-watchdog #687-A removed.)
  //
  // Stage 3 PR4 of the Worker split — see
  // docs/DesignsAndPlans/Stage3WorkerSplitPlan.md for the full
  // classification and migration sequencing.
  //
  // Custom domain (suggested): agent.vaipakam.com — operator
  // provisions the binding at first deploy. Worker name
  // `vaipakam-agent` stays post-cutover (deploy-switch identifier).
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "vaipakam-agent",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-23",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // ── keep_vars: the dashboard's plain-text vars survive a deploy ──
  //
  // Wrangler DELETES every var not present in this file before setting the
  // ones that are, so any value managed only in the dashboard is wiped by an
  // ordinary `wrangler deploy`. That is the hazard `--keep-vars` was being
  // added by hand at every call site to avoid — across runbooks, deploy
  // scripts, workflows and package scripts — which is an unbounded set of
  // spellings to keep correct, and #1995 spent 242 review findings proving it.
  //
  // Set HERE instead, the preservation is a property of the WORKER rather than
  // of each invocation: wrangler reads `config.keep_vars` on both the deploy
  // path and `versions upload`, so every spelling becomes safe at once,
  // including the ones nobody has written yet.
  //
  // Declared vars are still uploaded normally — this only suppresses the
  // DELETE of bindings absent from the config (wire field
  // `keep_bindings: ["plain_text","json"]`). Secrets were never at risk;
  // wrangler keeps those unconditionally.
  //
  // CONSEQUENCE, and it is the intended trade: a deploy can no longer REMOVE
  // a var. Deleting one is now a deliberate dashboard action.
  "keep_vars": true,

  // Cron — every minute. Internal pass-level guards (e.g. the
  // dailyOracleSnapshot's 00:00–00:09 UTC window check + last-day
  // D1 dedupe) make most ticks no-ops. Matches the existing
  // ops/hf-watcher cadence the agent's passes used to ride.
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["* * * * *"],
  },

  // D1 binding — `vaipakam-archive`, the STAGING database for the
  // 3-Worker split. NOT `vaipakam-alerts-db` (primary/production),
  // which this comment used to name — that misdirects migration and
  // incident work at production. The binding below is authoritative.
  //
  // All three Workers bind this same database id and all three WRITE
  // it. A D1 binding is database-scoped, not table-scoped, so the
  // per-Worker table lists below are a source convention, not an
  // enforced boundary (#1722). Tables this Worker touches:
  //   - `telegram_links`    (issue + consume during the Telegram
  //                          handshake — POST /link/telegram,
  //                          POST /unlink/telegram, POST /tg/webhook)
  //   - `user_thresholds`   (PUT /thresholds upserts)
  //   - `diag_errors`       (POST /diag/record + cron retention)
  //   - `diag_legal_holds`,
  //     `diag_legal_hold_audit` (T-075 hold place/lift + audit trail)
  //   - `support_tickets`   (POST /support/ticket + retention prune)
  //   - `loans`             (cross-Worker READ AND WRITE of the indexer's
  //                          loan rows: the periodic-pre-notify scan reads
  //                          them, then stamps `period_pre_notified_at` back
  //                          via UPDATE at `periodicPreNotify.ts:181-182`.
  //                          Listed as a read-only borrow before — the
  //                          understatement that matters, since it is another
  //                          Worker's table being mutated, which is exactly
  //                          the cross-Worker coupling #1722 is about)
  //
  // Names verified against apps/indexer/migrations/. An earlier list named
  // `link_codes`, `thresholds`, `loan_index_*` and `oracle_snapshot_*` —
  // the first three do not exist under any migration (the real tables are
  // `telegram_links`, `user_thresholds`, `loans`), and it omitted the
  // diagnostics and support tables this Worker actually writes.
  // These comments guide migration and incident work, so a wrong name here
  // costs someone time at the worst moment.
  //
  // `notify_state` is deliberately ABSENT, and a revision of this list
  // wrongly added it. `src/db.ts` does contain a SELECT and an INSERT
  // against that table, but their wrappers (`getNotifyState`,
  // `putNotifyState`) are unreferenced exports — no live agent path
  // imports either one, so the table is unreachable from this Worker.
  // The KEEPER owns `notify_state` (`apps/keeper/src/watcher.ts` calls
  // its own copies of both wrappers). The staging plan's call-graph
  // inventory excludes it for the same reason
  // (CloudflareStagingDeployPlan.md §4.3). Replacing a set of names that
  // do not exist with a name that exists but is unreachable is the same
  // defect in a new coat: it still overstates this Worker's data access.
  //
  // #1651: a `vpfi_buy_*` entry stood in that list, described as
  // buy-watchdog reconciliation reads. No such tables exist — no
  // migration under apps/indexer/migrations defines them, and no agent
  // code reads them — and #687-A removed the watchdog. Listing them
  // overstated this Worker's data access to anyone auditing the binding.
  //
  // Cross-Worker D1 sharing is the deliberate coupling that lets
  // the three Workers stay independent without a message-bus
  // layer (Stage 3 plan §5).
  // D1 binding — `vaipakam-archive` is the STAGING database for
  // the new 3-Worker split. Distinct from `vaipakam-alerts-db`
  // (primary, untouched). See
  // docs/DesignsAndPlans/CloudflareStagingDeployPlan.md §3.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "vaipakam-archive",
      "database_id": "3cffebf5-b652-4da7-953c-9e1d143ad2fe",
    },
  ],

  // R2 bucket — T-075 — private store for the legal documents
  // uploaded when a protocol admin places a diagnostics legal hold
  // (the e-signed order / scanned letter). Private by default; the
  // object key is the document's own SHA-256 (content-addressed).
  // The operator provisions the bucket before the legal-hold
  // endpoint is deployed; while the binding is absent, any
  // legal-hold action carrying a document returns 503.
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "DIAG_LEGAL_DOCS",
      "bucket_name": "vaipakam-legal-vault",
    },
  ],

  // Cloudflare built-in rate-limit bindings — one per upstream
  // service so a noisy /quote/0x caller can't drain the
  // /quote/1inch budget. 60 reqs / 60 seconds per IP each.
  // namespace_id values are arbitrary unique integers per Worker;
  // operator may renumber to match their account-level scheme.
  "unsafe": {
    "bindings": [
      {
        "name": "QUOTE_0X_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1001",
        "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        "name": "QUOTE_1INCH_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1002",
        "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        "name": "DIAG_RECORD_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1004",
        "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // #2013 — the signature-verification CHAIN PATH (ERC-1271/
        // 6492 checks against configured RPCs). Only requests that
        // fall past the free ECDSA fast path consume this, so a
        // human smart-account user spends ~1 per signed action;
        // the budget is sized for people and tight for a caller
        // spraying fake bodies to burn RPC quota.
        "name": "SIG_VERIFY_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1011",
        "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // T-086 step 14 — POST /opensea/listing is one call per
        // borrower post/update tx, so the budget is tight on
        // purpose. The endpoint exists so casual NFT buyers see
        // the listing on OpenSea's UI; abuse would just spam
        // OpenSea's API on our key.
        "name": "OPENSEA_LISTING_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1005",
        "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 },
      },
      // T-090 v1.1 GA (#430) — operator-registered rate-limit
      // binding for POST /intent/fusion/post. Not auto-declared
      // here because Cloudflare's deploy validates that every
      // `namespace_id` is registered with the account, and the
      // dev account this checks-in against has 1001-1005 only.
      //
      // ** Required, not optional, when ONEINCH_API_KEY is bound. **
      // The handler's half-activation gate returns
      // `503 rate-limit-not-configured` when the API key is bound
      // but this rate-limit binding is missing, so the bridge stays
      // in the half-activated reject state until BOTH are present
      // (Codex round-7 PR #430 P2). Skipping this step leaves the
      // API key spend ungated to Origin-spoofed callers; binding
      // the limiter without the key is harmless but doesn't
      // activate anything.
      //
      // Note (PR #436): the API key is the existing shared
      // ONEINCH_API_KEY entry in `secrets_store_secrets` below
      // (the same key `quoteProxy.ts` uses), with the LOP
      // Orderbook scope enabled at portal.1inch.dev. No separate
      // INTENT_FUSION_API_KEY secret is needed.
      //
      // To enable the gate in production:
      //   1. Register a new rate-limit namespace in the
      //      Cloudflare dashboard for this Worker.
      //   2. Add the binding back to this file with the chosen
      //      namespace_id under the name `INTENT_FUSION_POST_RATELIMIT`:
      //        { "name": "INTENT_FUSION_POST_RATELIMIT",
      //          "type": "ratelimit",
      //          "namespace_id": "<chosen-id>",
      //          "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 } }
      //
      // While this binding is absent (the as-merged state),
      // `checkRateLimit` no-ops because the no-API-key
      // short-circuit at the top of the handler returns the
      // queued-ack before any spend happens — the endpoint still
      // serves traffic. The Origin gate above remains in place
      // regardless.
      {
        "name": "INTENT_FUSION_POST_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "555",
        "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // T-086 Round-5 Block A (#313) — GET /opensea/collection/{slug}
        // is hit by the dapp once per post / update flow on a fee-
        // enforced collection (rare; only those collections need the
        // schedule fetch). Per-IP limit lets normal use through while
        // closing the "spoof an allowed Origin + iterate slugs to
        // drain quota" vector Codex P2 line 75 flagged on PR #324.
        "name": "OPENSEA_COLLECTION_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1006",
        "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // T-086 Round-5 Block C (#309 Mode B) —
        // GET /opensea/offers/{chainId}/{contract}/{tokenId}.
        // The dapp polls every ~30s while the loan card is open
        // so an active borrower can see fresh bids land. 60
        // requests/minute/IP gives ~2 calls per poll cycle of
        // headroom (one for the page, one for a background tab)
        // while still capping a scripted scraper.
        "name": "OPENSEA_OFFERS_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1007",
        "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // T-086 Round-5 Block C v1.1 (#334) — global upstream
        // rate-limit for the offers proxy, keyed by a constant
        // string ('opensea-offers-upstream') rather than per-IP.
        // The per-IP OPENSEA_OFFERS_RATELIMIT above bounds each
        // caller individually but doesn't cap aggregate calls
        // to the shared OPENSEA_API_KEY across many distinct
        // IPs polling hot tokens at a raised
        // OPENSEA_OFFERS_MAX_PAGES. At MAX_PAGES_CEILING = 24,
        // worst-case is 1 + 2 × 24 = 49 upstream RTs per
        // inbound. 60 requests/minute × 49 = 2,940 / min /
        // single-IP — fits the typical OpenSea API tier. The
        // aggregate cap below sets a hard ceiling at 3,000
        // upstream RTs / minute across ALL caller IPs combined,
        // so a multi-IP distributed scrape can't blow the
        // shared key's tier limit even at peak.
        "name": "OPENSEA_OFFERS_UPSTREAM_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1008",
        "simple": { "limit": 3000, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // T-086 Round-6 / Block D (#345) — per-IP rate-limit on
        // the GET /opensea/signed-offer/{chainId}/{contract}/{tokenId}/
        // {orderHash} endpoint. Hit by the dapp at Match-click
        // time to fetch the bidder's signed OpenSea Offer
        // (components + signature + SIP-7 extraData + resolvers)
        // for atomic match-rotation via
        // NFTPrepayListingAtomicFacet.matchOpenSeaOffer. One RTT
        // per inbound (no pagination), so per-IP gates aggregate
        // upstream load directly — no separate upstream binding
        // needed. 60 req/min/IP matches the per-IP shape of the
        // other agent proxies and is generous for the real
        // Match-click cadence (1 fetch per click, typically 1-2
        // clicks per loan).
        "name": "OPENSEA_SIGNED_OFFER_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1009",
        "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
      },
      {
        // #1040 phase 1 — per-IP gate on POST /support/ticket.
        // Human support requests arrive at human cadence; 5/min/IP
        // is generous for a real user and bounds abuse of the
        // shared D1 + the ops-Telegram notify.
        "name": "SUPPORT_TICKET_RATELIMIT",
        "type": "ratelimit",
        "namespace_id": "1010",
        "simple": { "limit": 5, "period": 60 },
      },
    ],
  },

  // T-078 — the agent's secrets are read from the account-level
  // Cloudflare Secrets Store (store `vaipakam-credentials`, id
  // 1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e), not per-Worker
  // `wrangler secret put`. Each binding is resolved asynchronously
  // once per cron tick / HTTP request in `resolveEnv()` (src/env.ts).
  // Local `wrangler dev` needs the same secrets created WITHOUT the
  // `--remote` flag.
  //
  // Agent carries the broadest secret surface of the three Workers:
  //   - RPC_*                — per-chain RPC URLs (carry API keys).
  //                            Consumers: every caller of
  //                            `getChainConfigs` (env.ts) plus
  //                            `intentFusionPost.rpcForChain`. Read
  //                            those for the per-consumer consequences
  //                            of a missing URL — they differ, and no
  //                            summary is kept here (#1651).
  //                            `RPC_ZKEVM` is omitted (Polygon zkEVM
  //                            out of scope).
  //                            #1651: previously justified by a
  //                            buy-watchdog that #687-A removed.
  //                            RPC_POLYGON / RPC_POLYGON_AMOY are
  //                            currently UNREACHABLE — no Polygon
  //                            deployment record exists — so they are
  //                            provisioned ahead of need. Only
  //                            RPC_POLYGON is agent-only; apps/indexer
  //                            binds RPC_POLYGON_AMOY as well.
  //   - TG_BOT_TOKEN         — Telegram bot (handshake + notifications).
  //   - PUSH_CHANNEL_PK      — Push channel signer.
  //   - ZEROEX_API_KEY,
  //     ONEINCH_API_KEY      — public quote-proxy aggregator keys.
  //   - OPENSEA_API_KEY      — T-086 step 14: OpenSea Listings API
  //                            key for `POST /opensea/listing`.
  //                            Server-side only.
  //   - DIAG_WALLET_HMAC_KEY — T-075: server secret keying the
  //                            per-wallet deletion hash. When unset,
  //                            connected-wallet capture skips writes
  //                            and the erasure endpoints 503.
  //
  // NOT migrated (no ON-CHAIN signing-key secret here; PUSH_CHANNEL_PK
  // IS an Ethereum key, see apps/agent/src/push.ts — Stage 3 architectural-
  // rebalance moved KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY + runDailyOracleSnapshot to
  // apps/keeper so the only Worker that holds an ON-CHAIN signing key is the
  // keeper; staging plan §2 least-privilege contract). The
  // `/diag/legal-hold` endpoint also has no secret — it checks the
  // caller's on-chain ADMIN_ROLE instead.
  "secrets_store_secrets": [
    {
      "binding": "RPC_BASE",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_BASE",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_ETH",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_ETH",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_ARB",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_ARB",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_OP",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_OP",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_BNB",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_BNB",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_POLYGON",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_POLYGON",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_SEPOLIA",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_SEPOLIA",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_BASE_SEPOLIA",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_BASE_SEPOLIA",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_ARB_SEPOLIA",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_ARB_SEPOLIA",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_OP_SEPOLIA",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_OP_SEPOLIA",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_POLYGON_AMOY",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_POLYGON_AMOY",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RPC_BNB_TESTNET",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "RPC_BNB_TESTNET",
    },
    {
      "binding": "TG_BOT_TOKEN",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "TG_BOT_TOKEN",
    },
    {
      "binding": "PUSH_CHANNEL_PK",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "PUSH_CHANNEL_PK",
    },
    {
      "binding": "ZEROEX_API_KEY",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "ZEROEX_API_KEY",
    },
    {
      "binding": "ONEINCH_API_KEY",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "ONEINCH_API_KEY",
    },
    // T-086 step 14 — OpenSea Listings API key powers
    // `POST /opensea/listing`. Server-side only; the dapp never
    // sees this key.
    {
      "binding": "OPENSEA_API_KEY",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "OPENSEA_API_KEY",
    },
    // T-090 v1.1 GA (#426 / #436) — the intent-Fusion bridge in
    // apps/agent/src/intentFusionPost.ts reads the SHARED
    // `ONEINCH_API_KEY` entry above (the same key quoteProxy.ts
    // already uses for the /quote/1inch proxy). The operator
    // enables the LOP Orderbook scope on that single key at
    // portal.1inch.dev; no separate INTENT_FUSION_API_KEY
    // secret is needed. The matching rate-limit binding is the
    // operator-action block under `unsafe.bindings` higher up
    // in this file (search for `INTENT_FUSION_POST_RATELIMIT`).
    {
      "binding": "DIAG_WALLET_HMAC_KEY",
      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e",
      "secret_name": "DIAG_WALLET_HMAC_KEY",
    },
    // ET-001 — no transaction-scan secret. The pre-sign transaction
    // preview is a frontend-only viem `eth_call` simulation (no API
    // key, so no server-side proxy). The Blockaid → GoPlus migration
    // was dropped — GoPlus's Transaction Simulation API is
    // mainnet-only (Eth/Base/BNB) and Vaipakam runs on testnets, so a
    // free `eth_call` is the all-chain choice. The GoPlus contextual
    // checks (ET-012/14/15/16) land later as their own cards and
    // re-add `GOPLUS_APP_KEY` / `GOPLUS_APP_SECRET` then.
  ],

  // Operator-managed vars (in `vars` below):
  //   - TG_BOT_USERNAME          — public bot handle.
  //   - FRONTEND_ORIGIN          — CSV of allowed CORS origins for
  //                                the frontend-facing endpoints.
  //                                An origin NOT on this list is not
  //                                rejected outright: resolveAllowedOrigin
  //                                falls back to the FIRST entry, so the
  //                                browser sees a mismatched
  //                                Access-Control-Allow-Origin and blocks
  //                                the call. Dropping a still-live origin
  //                                therefore breaks that site's agent
  //                                calls the next time this Worker is
  //                                deployed. Retire an origin only after
  //                                the surface behind it is actually gone.
  //   - DIAG_SAMPLE_RATE         — 0.0–1.0; default 1.0.
  //   - DIAG_RETENTION_DAYS      — int days; default 90.
  "vars": {
    "TG_BOT_USERNAME": "",
    // ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: frames.ts derives its link host from
    // `FRONTEND_ORIGIN.split(',')[0]`, and periodicPreNotify.ts builds
    // notification deep links the same way, so the FIRST entry must be a
    // host that actually SERVES those paths.
    //
    // It is `defi.` today, not `app.`, because `app.vaipakam.com` is not
    // bound yet (see apps/app/wrangler.jsonc). Entry zero and the paths
    // in frames.ts are ONE decision: the legacy app serves `/app` and
    // `/nft-verifier`, the new one serves `/` and `/nft`. Move them
    // together at cutover — same coupling `appUrl`'s APP_TARGET models
    // on the marketing side — or the links break in whichever direction
    // you half-changed.
    //
    // `defi.` and `alpha02.` are RETAINED past the #1854 rename on
    // purpose: their Workers still serve real users until the operator
    // converts them to redirects. Remove them in the same change that
    // retires those hosts, not before. (#1958 Codex F5.)
    // The trailing `*.workers.dev` entry is the PRE-CUTOVER REVIEW target.
    // CLAUDE.md requires a live review of the deployment just made, and
    // until `app.vaipakam.com` is bound that deployment is only reachable
    // on its workers.dev URL. Without it here, every agent-backed flow
    // (alerts, support ticket, diagnostics/erasure) fails CORS on the
    // mandated review target, so the review cannot exercise them. It is a
    // single exact origin, not a wildcard. REMOVE IT once the custom
    // domain is bound — see step 5 of the cutover checklist in
    // apps/www/src/lib/appUrl.ts.
    "FRONTEND_ORIGIN": "https://defi.vaipakam.com,https://app.vaipakam.com,https://labs.vaipakam.com,https://www.vaipakam.com,https://vaipakam.com,https://alpha02.vaipakam.com,https://vaipakam-app.dawn-fire-139e.workers.dev",
    "DIAG_SAMPLE_RATE": "1.0",
    "DIAG_RETENTION_DAYS": "90",
  },

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
  },
}
